/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Business stories and strategy */
WB.L("pricing-psychology", "business", "beginner", "The hidden psychology of prices", "Why does almost everything cost $9.99, and why does a menu include a dish nobody orders? Prices are quietly designed to steer your choices.",
  [
    ["The magic of .99", "Prices ending in 9 feel cheaper than they are because we read from left to right.", "Walk into any Australian supermarket and you will see it everywhere: $4.99, $19.95, $299. A price of $4.99 is only one cent less than $5, yet it often feels like 'four dollars and something'. Researchers call this the left-digit effect. Our brains read numbers from left to right and give the first digit far more weight than the rest, so a jump from 4 to 5 feels bigger than it really is. Studies of sales data have found that prices ending in 9 can lift demand, although the effect varies by product. Luxury shops often do the opposite, using round prices like $500 to signal quality, because a price that looks discounted can make an expensive item feel cheap."],
    ["Anchors set the scene", "The first number you see shapes what every later number feels like.", "Imagine a jacket marked 'Was $400, now $199'. Whether or not anyone ever paid $400, that first figure works as an anchor. Psychologists have shown again and again that people judge values relative to a reference point, even an irrelevant one. In famous experiments by Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman in the 1970s, people's estimates were nudged by a number from a spun wheel. Retailers use anchors constantly. A restaurant may list a very expensive seafood platter at the top of the menu, not because it sells many, but because it makes the mid-priced dishes look reasonable. In Australia, consumer law says 'was' prices must be genuine, and the ACCC has taken action against businesses that use misleading reference prices."],
    ["The decoy that nobody buys", "Adding an unattractive option can make another option look like a bargain.", "Behavioural economist Dan Ariely described a striking example from an old subscription offer by The Economist magazine. It offered web access alone for $59, print alone for $125, and print plus web for $125. Nobody in their right mind chooses print alone when the bundle costs the same, so why include it? Because it makes the bundle look like a steal. When Ariely tested the offer with students, most chose the bundle. When he removed the useless print-only option, far more chose the cheaper web-only deal. This is called the decoy effect. You will see it in cinema popcorn sizes, coffee cups and phone plans, where a middle option is priced to push you up to the largest one."],
    ["Same product, different prices", "Many businesses charge different customers different prices for almost the same thing.", "Two people sitting side by side on a plane may have paid wildly different fares. Economists call this price discrimination, and it is often perfectly legal. The idea is to charge each customer close to what they are willing to pay. Airlines do it with booking dates and flexible tickets. Cinemas do it with cheap Tuesday sessions. Software companies offer student discounts, knowing students have little money now but may become loyal paying customers later. Coupons and loyalty apps work in a similar way: people who bother to collect points or hunt for codes tend to be more price sensitive, so they get a lower price while everyone else pays full price. The product barely changes, but the price fits the customer."],
    ["Free is a powerful word", "A price of zero feels completely different from a very low price.", "There is something special about free. Shoppers will often choose a free item over a much better deal that costs a small amount, because zero removes any sense of risk or loss. Businesses have built entire models around this. Many apps are free to download and earn money from a small share of users who pay for extra features, an approach called freemium. Supermarkets offer free samples because tasting something creates a small feeling of obligation and lets you discover a product you might not try. Free shipping thresholds are another trick. If delivery is free over $50 and your cart sits at $43, many people add something extra rather than pay a few dollars for postage."],
    ["Subscriptions and the pain of paying", "Spreading costs into small regular payments makes spending feel less painful.", "Paying hurts a little. Researchers have described a 'pain of paying' that is sharper with cash than with cards, and duller still when money leaves automatically. That is one reason subscriptions have spread from newspapers to software, cars, meal kits and even dog food. A price of $15 a month feels lighter than $180 a year, even though it adds up to the same amount. Subscriptions also benefit from inertia: once you sign up, you have to make an active choice to stop. Many people keep paying for services they rarely use. A quick habit worth building is to review your bank statement every few months and list every recurring charge, then cancel anything you would not sign up for again today."],
    ["Seeing through the tricks", "Knowing the techniques helps you compare the real cost of things.", "None of these techniques are secret, and once you know them they lose some of their power. A useful defence is unit pricing. Large Australian supermarkets are required to display unit prices, such as the cost per 100 grams, which makes it much easier to compare a big pack with a small one or a branded product with a home brand. Another defence is to decide what something is worth to you before you look at the price tag, so an anchor cannot do your thinking for you. The ACCC has also cracked down on drip pricing, where extra fees appear only at the end of a purchase. If a price seems misleading, the ACCC website explains your rights and how to report it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the left-digit effect?", ["Giving the first digit of a price extra weight", "Preferring prices with more digits", "Rounding all prices up in your head", "Ignoring prices that end in zero"], 0, "We read prices left to right, so $4.99 feels closer to $4 than to $5."],
    [1, "Why might a restaurant list a very expensive dish at the top of its menu?", ["Because it sells the most", "To act as an anchor that makes other dishes seem reasonable", "Because the law requires it", "To hide the cheapest dish"], 1, "An expensive anchor makes mid-priced options look like better value."],
    [2, "In The Economist example, what did the print-only option do?", ["It was the most popular choice", "It was a printing mistake", "It acted as a decoy that made the bundle look like a bargain", "It made the web-only option more popular"], 2, "Nobody wanted print-only, but its presence pushed people towards the bundle."],
    [4, "What is a 'freemium' business model?", ["A product sold only to premium customers", "A free trial that ends after one day", "A government subsidy for apps", "A free product that earns money from users who pay for extras"], 3, "Freemium apps are free to use, with paid features for those who want more."],
    [6, "How does unit pricing help shoppers?", ["It shows the cost per standard amount, making comparisons easier", "It guarantees the lowest price", "It removes GST from groceries", "It shows which brand is healthiest"], 0, "Unit prices like cost per 100 grams let you compare different pack sizes fairly."]
  ]);

WB.L("power-of-brands", "business", "beginner", "Why we pay more for a name", "In blind taste tests, people often cannot tell their favourite cola from its rival. So why will they happily pay more for the logo?",
  [
    ["Born on a cow's hide", "The word brand comes from burning a mark of ownership onto livestock and goods.", "Long before logos, farmers burned marks into the hides of cattle so everyone knew whose animals they were. Potters, brickmakers and stonemasons in the ancient world stamped their work for similar reasons. The modern brand grew out of the Industrial Revolution, when goods began travelling far from the people who made them. If you bought flour from a neighbour, you knew who to blame if it was bad. If it came from a factory hundreds of kilometres away, a trusted name on the packet did that job instead. In 1876 the Bass brewery's red triangle became the first trade mark registered under a new British law, and it later appeared in a famous painting by Edouard Manet."],
    ["A shortcut for the brain", "Brands save us time and reduce the risk of a bad choice.", "A typical large supermarket stocks many thousands of products. If you had to research every one, a weekly shop would take days. Brands act as mental shortcuts. When you see a familiar name, you can assume the product will be roughly the same as last time, and that someone will be accountable if it is not. That sense of predictability is worth paying for, especially when a mistake would be costly or embarrassing. It is why travellers in a strange city sometimes choose a global coffee chain over a local cafe: they know exactly what they will get. Economists describe this as reducing uncertainty, and much of what a brand sells is simply confidence."],
    ["The cola experiment", "Knowing the brand can literally change how something tastes to us.", "In the 1970s Pepsi ran the Pepsi Challenge, a marketing campaign built on blind taste tests in which many people preferred Pepsi. Yet Coca-Cola remained the bigger seller. In 2004 a team of neuroscientists explored why. When people tasted the drinks without knowing which was which, their preferences were fairly evenly split. When they were told they were drinking Coke, more of them said they preferred it, and brain scans showed extra activity in areas linked to memory and emotion. In other words, the brand was not just a label on the can. A lifetime of advertising, memories and associations had become part of the experience of drinking it. Brands work partly in our heads, and that is still real to us."],
    ["Status and signalling", "Some brands are bought mainly to tell others something about us.", "In 1899 the economist Thorstein Veblen coined the phrase 'conspicuous consumption' to describe wealthy people spending lavishly to display their status. Luxury brands still trade on this idea. A handbag with a famous logo carries the same things as an unbranded bag, but it also sends a message. For some luxury goods, raising the price can even increase demand, because the high price is part of the appeal. Economists sometimes call these Veblen goods. Signalling is not only about wealth. People choose brands to show they are outdoorsy, ethical, sporty or creative. A sticker on a laptop or a logo on a pair of runners can quietly announce which group you feel you belong to."],
    ["When a brand becomes the word", "Brands that become too successful can lose their legal protection.", "Escalator, thermos and trampoline all began life as brand names. Over time people used them to describe any product of that type, and in some countries the owners lost the exclusive right to the name. Courts call this genericide. In the United States, Otis lost its trade mark on 'escalator' in 1950. Companies fight hard to avoid this fate. That is why some firms insist on phrases like 'Band-Aid brand adhesive bandages' in their ads. In Australia, Esky is a registered trade mark, even though many people use it for any portable cooler. The irony is painful for the owners: becoming a household word is the ultimate marketing success, yet it can wipe out the legal value of the name."],
    ["The rise of home brands", "Supermarket own brands have shown that many shoppers will switch if quality is close enough.", "For decades, supermarket own brands had a reputation as plain and cheap. That changed as retailers realised they could make good margins with their own labels. When the German discount chain Aldi arrived in Australia in 2001, it built its business largely on own-label products sold at low prices, often in packaging that looks similar to the big brands. Coles and Woolworths expanded their own ranges in response. Many home brand items are made in the same kinds of factories as branded goods, and sometimes by the same manufacturers. This has squeezed some well-known brands, which now have to justify their higher prices with genuine quality, innovation or emotional appeal rather than familiarity alone."],
    ["Trust takes years, loses in days", "A brand is a promise, and broken promises can destroy enormous value quickly.", "Because a brand is really a reputation, it is fragile. Companies spend decades building trust that a single scandal can damage. When products turn out to be unsafe, or when a company is caught misleading customers, sales and share prices can fall sharply. Some brands recover by acting quickly and honestly, recalling products and explaining what went wrong. Others never fully regain trust. This is why big firms watch social media so closely and why customer service matters so much: every interaction adds to or subtracts from the brand. For shoppers, the lesson is useful too. A brand tells you what a company wants you to feel. It is worth checking whether the product actually earns the extra price you pay."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did brands become more important during the Industrial Revolution?", ["Goods travelled far from the people who made them", "Governments banned unbranded goods", "Factories could not print labels before then", "Cattle farming increased"], 0, "When buyers no longer knew the maker personally, a trusted name filled the gap."],
    [2, "What did the 2004 cola study suggest?", ["Coke and Pepsi are chemically identical", "Nobody can taste the difference between colas", "Knowing the brand changed people's preferences and brain activity", "Blind tests always favour the bigger brand"], 2, "When people knew they were drinking Coke, more preferred it and their brains responded differently."],
    [3, "What did Thorstein Veblen mean by conspicuous consumption?", ["Buying only what you need", "Spending lavishly to display status", "Shopping in public markets", "Choosing the cheapest option"], 1, "Veblen used the phrase for spending designed to show off wealth."],
    [4, "What is 'genericide'?", ["A ban on generic medicines", "When a brand name becomes the everyday word and loses protection", "A price war between home brands", "Destroying unsold stock"], 1, "Names like escalator lost legal protection after becoming the common word."],
    [5, "When did Aldi open in Australia?", ["1971", "1988", "2001", "2015"], 2, "Aldi arrived in 2001 and grew with low-priced own-label products."]
  ]);

WB.L("economic-moats", "business", "advanced", "Moats: why some companies stay on top", "Most successful businesses attract copycats that eat away their profits. A few seem untouchable for decades. The difference is what investors call a moat.",
  [
    ["The castle and the moat", "A moat is any lasting advantage that stops rivals from stealing a company's profits.", "Picture a medieval castle full of treasure. The treasure is a company's profits, and every competitor nearby would love to storm the walls. Investor Warren Buffett popularised the idea that the best businesses are protected by a wide 'economic moat'. In ordinary markets, high profits are a signal to others. If a cafe makes a fortune, new cafes open next door, prices fall and profits shrink back to normal. Economists call this competition doing its job. A moat is something that interrupts that process, allowing a company to keep earning unusually high returns for many years. Spotting a real moat is hard, and mistaking temporary success for a lasting advantage is one of the most common errors in business and investing."],
    ["Network effects", "Some products become more valuable as more people use them.", "A telephone is useless if you are the only person who owns one, and more useful with every new person who joins the network. This is a network effect, and it can create some of the strongest moats in business. Card payment networks are a classic example. Shops accept a card because customers carry it, and customers carry it because shops accept it. Online marketplaces and social media platforms work in a similar way. A new rival might build a better product, but it struggles to persuade everyone to switch at the same time. Network effects help explain why many digital markets end up dominated by one or two giants. They also explain why regulators around the world watch these platforms so closely."],
    ["Switching costs", "When changing to a rival is painful, customers tend to stay put even if they grumble.", "Think about how hard it would be for a large company to change its accounting software. Years of records, trained staff, custom reports and connected systems would all need to move. Even if a competitor were cheaper, the cost and risk of switching might be too high. These switching costs form a powerful moat. They are not always about money. Changing banks means updating direct debits. Leaving a phone ecosystem means losing apps, settings and familiar habits. Businesses often deepen switching costs deliberately with loyalty schemes, bundled services and proprietary file formats. For customers, it is worth noticing when convenience has quietly turned into dependence, because that is exactly when a company has the most power over price."],
    ["Cost advantages and scale", "Being able to produce more cheaply than anyone else is a moat of its own.", "Some companies win simply because they can do things more cheaply than rivals and pass part of the saving to customers. Size often helps. A huge retailer can negotiate lower prices from suppliers, spread advertising across thousands of stores and invest in efficient logistics that a small competitor cannot afford. Economists call these economies of scale. Location can matter too. A quarry close to a city has a lasting advantage, because rock is heavy and expensive to transport. Some cost advantages come from unique processes developed over decades. The catch is that cost advantages can disappear if a rival finds a cleverer method or new technology, so companies relying on them must keep improving constantly."],
    ["Intangible assets", "Brands, patents and licences can keep competitors legally or emotionally at bay.", "Some moats cannot be touched. A patent gives an inventor the exclusive right to an invention for a limited time, usually up to 20 years, in exchange for publicly disclosing how it works. Drug companies rely heavily on patents, and their revenues can fall sharply when a major patent expires and cheaper generic versions arrive. Government licences can also create moats, for example where only a few companies are permitted to operate in a market. Brands are the third kind of intangible moat. A brand that customers trust, or that lets a company charge more than rivals for a similar product, is a genuine economic advantage. Not every famous brand has this power, though. Familiarity alone does not always translate into pricing power."],
    ["Moats can dry up", "Technology and changing habits can drain even the widest moat.", "History is full of companies that looked unbeatable. In the late 2000s Nokia sold more mobile phones than anyone, and BlackBerry was the device of choice for business. Within a few years smartphones built around touchscreens and app stores had pushed both aside. Newspapers once had a powerful moat in classified advertising: if you wanted to sell a car or find a job, you went to the paper with the most readers. The internet drained that moat remarkably quickly. These stories show that moats are not permanent. They need constant maintenance, and they are vulnerable to shifts that make the old advantage irrelevant. A company that grows complacent behind its walls may not notice the water level falling."],
    ["Thinking like an analyst", "Asking 'what stops a rival copying this?' is a simple test of any business.", "You can use moat thinking whenever you look at a business, whether it is a local bakery or a global tech giant. Ask a simple question: if a well-funded competitor tried to copy this company tomorrow, what would stop them? If the honest answer is 'not much', high profits are unlikely to last. Analysts also look for evidence, such as steady returns on capital over many years, loyal customers and the ability to raise prices without losing sales. Remember that a great company is not automatically a great investment if its share price already reflects all that strength. This is general information rather than financial advice, and moneysmart.gov.au has helpful guidance if you are thinking about investing."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "In business, what is an economic moat?", ["A company's cash reserves", "A lasting advantage that protects profits from competitors", "A legal ban on new competitors", "A water supply contract"], 1, "A moat is any durable advantage that stops rivals eroding a company's profits."],
    [1, "Why do card payment networks have strong network effects?", ["Shops accept cards customers carry, and customers carry cards shops accept", "They are owned by the government", "They charge no fees", "They have patents on plastic cards"], 0, "Each side of the network makes it more valuable to the other."],
    [2, "Which is the best example of a switching cost?", ["A competitor lowering its prices", "A new product launch", "The effort of moving years of records to new accounting software", "A rise in interest rates"], 2, "When changing providers is costly or risky, customers tend to stay."],
    [4, "What usually happens when a major drug patent expires?", ["The drug becomes illegal", "The company gets a permanent licence", "Prices rise sharply", "Cheaper generic versions arrive and revenues can fall"], 3, "Patents are temporary, so competition arrives when they expire."],
    [5, "What lesson do Nokia and BlackBerry illustrate?", ["Moats can be drained by new technology", "Brands never matter", "Big companies always win", "Phones are a bad business"], 0, "Both lost their dominant positions when smartphones changed the market."]
  ]);

WB.L("kodak-disruption", "business", "intermediate", "Kodak and the innovator's dilemma", "In 1975 a young Kodak engineer built the world's first digital camera. Nearly four decades later, Kodak filed for bankruptcy. How does a company invent the future and still lose it?",
  [
    ["You press the button", "Kodak made photography easy and became one of the world's great companies.", "In the 1880s George Eastman set out to make photography simple enough for anyone. His first Kodak camera came loaded with film. When you had finished the roll, you sent the whole camera back to the company, which developed the pictures and returned the camera reloaded. The slogan was 'You press the button, we do the rest'. Over the following century Kodak became a household name. Its yellow boxes were sold all over the world, and the phrase 'a Kodak moment' entered everyday speech. For much of the twentieth century the company dominated the film market in the United States, and it employed many tens of thousands of people at its peak."],
    ["Selling the film, not the camera", "Kodak's profits came mostly from film and processing, which every photo needed.", "Kodak's business model is sometimes compared to razors and blades. Cameras could be sold cheaply, because the real money came from what customers bought again and again: film, chemicals, paper and processing. Every holiday, birthday and wedding meant more rolls of film. This was a wonderful business. It produced high margins year after year, and Kodak's scale, brand and chemical expertise made it very hard for rivals to compete. But it also created a trap. The company's entire structure, from its factories to its sales teams to its managers' bonuses, was built around a product that depended on chemistry. Anything that threatened film threatened the heart of the company's profits."],
    ["The camera nobody wanted", "Kodak invented the digital camera but had little reason to push it.", "In 1975 a Kodak engineer named Steven Sasson built a prototype digital camera. It was about the size of a toaster, weighed several kilograms and took around 23 seconds to record a single black and white image, at a resolution of about 0.01 megapixels, onto a cassette tape. Viewing the picture required a special playback system and a television. Sasson later recalled that managers found it interesting but were not sure why anyone would want to look at photos on a TV. That reaction was reasonable at the time. The technology was crude and expensive. The problem was what happened over the following decades, as digital improved rapidly while Kodak's core business still depended on film."],
    ["The innovator's dilemma", "Successful companies often ignore new technologies because they look small and unprofitable at first.", "In 1997 Harvard professor Clayton Christensen published The Innovator's Dilemma. He studied industries such as computer disk drives and noticed a pattern. Well-run companies listened carefully to their best customers and improved their existing products. New technologies usually began worse, cheaper and aimed at small or unfashionable markets. Sensible managers ignored them, because they offered lower profits and their main customers did not want them. But the new technology kept improving until it was good enough for everyone, and by then the old leaders were too far behind. Christensen called this disruption. The painful insight was that the leaders did not fail through stupidity. They failed by doing what good managers are trained to do."],
    ["Too little, too late", "Kodak did move into digital, but digital could never replace film's profits.", "Kodak was not blind to digital. It invested heavily, held valuable patents and for a time was one of the leading sellers of digital cameras in the United States. The trouble was that digital cameras were a competitive hardware business with thin margins, nothing like the steady flood of profits from film. Then smartphones arrived and made separate cameras unnecessary for most people. Photos were shared online rather than printed, so the processing business shrank too. Kodak cut tens of thousands of jobs and sold off divisions, but in January 2012 it filed for bankruptcy protection in the United States. It later emerged as a much smaller company focused on commercial printing and other specialised areas."],
    ["Fujifilm took another road", "Kodak's great rival survived by finding new uses for its chemical know-how.", "Japan's Fujifilm faced exactly the same collapse in film sales, yet it survived far better. Its leaders asked what else the company was good at. Making film involves sophisticated chemistry, including handling collagen and preventing the fading caused by oxidation. Fujifilm applied that knowledge to new markets such as medical imaging, pharmaceuticals, specialised materials and even skincare. It also bought other businesses to speed up the change. The shift was painful and involved major restructuring, but it worked. The contrast is often used in business schools. Both companies saw digital coming. The difference lay partly in how willing each was to treat its old core business as something that would not last."],
    ["Spotting disruption today", "Asking whether a cheap, 'inferior' newcomer is improving fast can reveal future disruption.", "Kodak's story repeats across industries. Video rental chain Blockbuster reportedly had the chance to buy a young Netflix around 2000 and declined, before filing for bankruptcy in 2010. Taxi companies underestimated ride-sharing apps. Traditional media underestimated online platforms. The lesson is not that big companies are foolish, but that success makes it hard to embrace something that eats your own profits. Some firms tackle this by creating separate teams free to compete with the main business. For everyday thinking, a useful question is this: is there a cheaper, simpler product that experts dismiss as not good enough, but which keeps getting better every year? If so, it may be worth watching closely."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "Where did most of Kodak's profit come from?", ["Selling expensive cameras", "Film, chemicals and processing", "Television advertising", "Digital camera patents only"], 1, "Like razors and blades, the recurring sales of film drove the profits."],
    [2, "How did Kodak's 1975 digital camera prototype store images?", ["On a memory card", "On a cassette tape", "On film", "On a floppy disk"], 1, "Sasson's prototype recorded its low-resolution image onto a cassette tape."],
    [3, "According to Clayton Christensen, why do good companies often miss disruptive technologies?", ["Their managers are lazy", "They lack money", "The new technology starts out worse and less profitable, so they rationally ignore it", "Governments protect them"], 2, "Disruptive products begin small and unattractive, so sensible managers focus elsewhere."],
    [4, "When did Kodak file for bankruptcy protection?", ["1975", "1997", "2005", "2012"], 3, "Kodak filed for bankruptcy protection in January 2012."],
    [5, "How did Fujifilm survive the decline of film?", ["By applying its chemistry expertise to new markets", "By making film more expensive", "By merging with Kodak", "By leaving photography entirely in the 1970s"], 0, "Fujifilm moved into medical imaging, materials and skincare, among other areas."]
  ]);

WB.L("ford-toyota-production", "business", "intermediate", "Ford, Toyota and the art of making things", "Two car companies, half a world apart, changed how almost everything is made, from your phone to your runners. Their ideas still shape factories, hospitals and offices today.",
  [
    ["A car for the many", "Henry Ford wanted a car ordinary working families could afford.", "In the early 1900s cars were expensive toys for the wealthy, often built by hand by skilled craftsmen. Henry Ford had a different vision. In 1908 his company launched the Model T, a simple, tough car designed for rough country roads and ordinary buyers. To make it cheap enough, Ford needed to build it far faster and more efficiently than anyone had before. His engineers studied every step of production, breaking the work into small, repeatable tasks. They borrowed ideas from many places, including the overhead conveyor lines used in Chicago meatpacking plants, where carcasses moved past workers who each made the same cut. Ford's team reversed the idea: instead of taking something apart, they would put a car together."],
    ["The moving assembly line", "Bringing the work to the worker slashed the time needed to build a car.", "In 1913 Ford introduced a moving assembly line at its Highland Park plant in Michigan. Instead of workers walking around a stationary car, the car moved past them on a line, and each worker repeated one task. The results were astonishing. The time needed to assemble a Model T chassis fell from more than 12 hours to about an hour and a half. As production soared, the price of the car fell steadily, and by the 1920s millions of Model Ts were on the roads. Ford famously offered limited choice to keep things simple, and for years the car came only in black. Mass production had arrived, and factories around the world rushed to copy it."],
    ["The five dollar day", "Ford doubled wages to keep workers on a gruelling, repetitive line.", "The assembly line had a human cost. The work was monotonous and exhausting, and many workers quit after only a short time. Staff turnover was so high that Ford was constantly hiring and training new people. In 1914 the company made a dramatic announcement: it would pay many workers five dollars a day, roughly double the typical wage for factory work at the time, along with a shorter working day. Crowds of job seekers gathered outside the factory. The policy came with conditions, and Ford's company inspected aspects of some workers' private lives. But it reduced turnover sharply, and it helped create a workforce that could afford to buy the very cars it was building."],
    ["Toyota's problem", "Post-war Japan needed a way to make many different cars in small numbers.", "After World War II, Toyota faced a very different situation. Japan was recovering from devastation, money was scarce and the domestic market wanted many types of vehicle in modest quantities. Ford's model of huge runs of identical cars, supported by enormous stockpiles of parts, simply did not fit. Over the following decades, engineers led by Taiichi Ohno developed what became known as the Toyota Production System. Its central aim was to eliminate waste of every kind: overproduction, waiting, unnecessary movement, defects and excess inventory. Ohno later said he drew inspiration from American supermarkets, where shelves were restocked only as customers took items away. Why not run a factory the same way, making things only as they were needed?"],
    ["Just in time and stop the line", "Toyota made parts only when needed and gave every worker the power to halt production.", "Two ideas sit at the heart of Toyota's system. The first is just in time: each step of production pulls parts from the step before only when it needs them, often signalled by simple cards called kanban. This keeps stockpiles small and exposes problems quickly. The second is sometimes called jidoka, or automation with a human touch. Machines are designed to stop when something goes wrong, and workers can pull a cord, known as an andon, to halt the line if they spot a defect. To outsiders this seemed crazy. Stopping a whole production line is expensive. But fixing the cause of a problem immediately prevents thousands of faulty cars from being built later."],
    ["Kaizen: always a little better", "Small, constant improvements from everyone can add up to a huge advantage.", "Toyota also encouraged kaizen, a Japanese word usually translated as continuous improvement. Rather than relying only on managers and experts, the company expected workers on the factory floor to suggest ways to make their own jobs safer, simpler and less wasteful. One well-known problem solving technique is the 'five whys'. When something goes wrong, you ask why it happened, then ask why again about the answer, and keep going until you reach the root cause rather than the surface symptom. By the 1980s Toyota's cars had a strong reputation for reliability, and Western carmakers were studying its methods closely. A 1990 book called The Machine That Changed the World helped spread the label 'lean production'."],
    ["Lean everywhere, and its limits", "Lean thinking spread far beyond car factories, but efficiency has trade-offs.", "Lean ideas are now used in hospitals, software companies, warehouses and government offices. Agile software teams, for example, borrow the idea of small batches and quick feedback. But lean has limits. Keeping very little inventory works beautifully when supply chains run smoothly, and badly when they break. After the 2011 earthquake and tsunami in Japan, and again during the COVID-19 pandemic, shortages of key parts such as computer chips halted car production around the world. Many companies have since decided to hold larger buffers of critical components. The deeper lesson from Ford and Toyota is not a single method. It is that the way work is organised can matter as much as the technology itself."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "What was revolutionary about Ford's 1913 assembly line?", ["It used robots for every task", "The car moved past workers, each doing one task", "It built cars in any colour", "It was the first factory with electricity"], 1, "Bringing the work to the worker dramatically cut assembly time."],
    [2, "Why did Ford introduce the five dollar day?", ["To reduce very high staff turnover", "Because the government required it", "To raise car prices", "To replace workers with machines"], 0, "Turnover was so high that doubling pay made economic sense."],
    [3, "What inspired Taiichi Ohno's approach to restocking parts?", ["Military supply depots", "Japanese rice farms", "Ford's Highland Park plant", "American supermarkets"], 3, "Supermarkets restocked shelves only as customers took items, an idea Toyota adapted."],
    [4, "What is an andon cord used for?", ["Signalling the end of a shift", "Ordering new parts", "Letting a worker stop the line when there is a problem", "Measuring worker speed"], 2, "Any worker can halt production so a defect is fixed at its source."],
    [6, "What is a key weakness of keeping very little inventory?", ["It makes factories too large", "Production can halt when supply chains are disrupted", "It raises wages", "It makes cars less reliable"], 1, "Events like the 2011 tsunami and the chip shortage exposed this risk."]
  ]);

WB.L("monopolies-competition", "business", "beginner", "Monopolies and why competition matters", "The board game Monopoly was invented to warn people about monopolies. More than a century later, governments are still wrestling with the same problem.",
  [
    ["A game with a lesson", "Monopoly began as a warning about what happens when one player owns everything.", "In 1904 an American woman named Lizzie Magie patented a board game called The Landlord's Game. She designed it to show how concentrated ownership of land could make a few people rich while bankrupting everyone else. Players moved around a board buying property and charging rent, and the game ended when one player had crushed the rest. Versions spread informally for years before a game called Monopoly was sold by Parker Brothers in the 1930s, and for a long time Charles Darrow was credited as its sole inventor. The irony is striking. A game meant to criticise monopolies became a celebration of them. But it captured a real truth: when one player controls the market, everyone else loses."],
    ["What a monopoly is", "A monopoly exists when one seller dominates a market and faces little real competition.", "Strictly, a monopoly is a market with a single seller. In practice, economists and regulators worry about any firm with enough market power to raise prices or cut quality without losing many customers. When you have no real alternative, the seller can charge more than it costs to produce something and still keep your business. Competition works in the opposite direction. If one cafe overcharges, you walk to the next one, so each has to keep prices fair and quality high. Markets with only two or three big players, called duopolies and oligopolies, can sit somewhere in between. They may compete fiercely, or they may settle into a comfortable pattern where nobody rocks the boat."],
    ["Rockefeller's oil empire", "Standard Oil became so powerful that the United States government broke it apart.", "In the late 1800s John D. Rockefeller built Standard Oil into one of the most powerful companies in history. It grew partly through genuine efficiency, cutting the cost of refining kerosene for lamps. But it also used aggressive tactics, including secret deals with railways for cheaper transport and pushing rivals to sell or face ruin. At its height Standard Oil controlled the great majority of oil refining in the United States. Public anger led to new antitrust laws, and in 1911 the US Supreme Court ordered the company to be broken up into dozens of separate firms. Some of those pieces eventually grew into giants of their own, which later became part of companies like ExxonMobil and Chevron."],
    ["Natural monopolies", "Some services work best with one provider, so governments regulate them closely.", "Not every monopoly is a villain. Some markets are natural monopolies, where it makes no sense to have competing networks. Imagine three different companies each laying their own water pipes down your street, or building separate sets of electricity poles. The cost would be enormous and pointless. In these cases one network is usually allowed to operate, but its prices and service are regulated, or it is owned by the government. In Australia, independent regulators oversee the poles and wires that carry electricity and set limits on what network owners can charge. Retailers then compete to sell electricity across those shared networks. The goal is to keep the efficiency of a single network while protecting customers from monopoly prices."],
    ["Australia's competition watchdog", "The ACCC enforces laws against cartels and conduct that harms competition.", "In Australia, competition law is mainly set out in the Competition and Consumer Act 2010, and enforced by the Australian Competition and Consumer Commission, the ACCC. It is illegal for competitors to form a cartel, secretly agreeing to fix prices, share out customers or rig bids, and serious cartel conduct can be a criminal offence. Companies with substantial market power are also banned from using it in ways that substantially lessen competition. Since 2026, larger mergers must also be notified to the ACCC and cannot go ahead without its approval, so it can block a deal that would leave customers with too little choice. Australia's relatively small population means many markets have only a few big players, from supermarkets to airlines, so these questions come up often in public debate."],
    ["Monopolies in the digital age", "Tech platforms raise new questions about how to measure power when products are free.", "Older competition laws were mostly built around prices. If a monopoly charged too much, that was the harm. But many of today's most powerful companies offer products that are free to use, such as search engines and social media. They earn money from advertising and data, which makes power harder to measure. Network effects mean the biggest platforms tend to keep growing. Regulators in many countries are now testing new approaches. In 2024 a United States court ruled that Google had illegally maintained a monopoly in online search. The European Union has passed laws placing special obligations on the largest digital gatekeepers. Australia has also examined digital platforms closely, including through long-running ACCC inquiries."],
    ["Why it matters to you", "Competition affects the prices you pay, the quality you get and the new ideas that reach you.", "It is easy to think of competition law as something for lawyers and economists, but it touches daily life. When airlines compete on a route, fares tend to fall. When a new phone company enters the market, existing ones often cut prices or add data. Competition also drives innovation, because firms that stop improving lose customers to those that do. Without it, a dominant business can grow lazy, knowing customers have nowhere else to go. You play a part too. Shopping around, switching providers and supporting new entrants all keep markets healthy. If you suspect businesses are colluding or behaving unfairly, the ACCC website explains how competition law works and how to make a report."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the original purpose of Lizzie Magie's Landlord's Game?", ["To teach children to count money", "To show the dangers of concentrated land ownership", "To advertise property developers", "To celebrate Rockefeller"], 1, "Magie designed it as a warning about monopoly power over land."],
    [2, "What happened to Standard Oil in 1911?", ["It went bankrupt", "It merged with its rivals", "The US Supreme Court ordered it broken up", "It moved to Europe"], 2, "The court ordered Standard Oil split into many separate companies."],
    [3, "Why are electricity poles and wires often a natural monopoly?", ["Duplicating the network would be wasteful and costly", "The technology is secret", "Customers prefer a single brand", "Only one company knows how to build them"], 0, "Building competing networks along every street would be enormously expensive."],
    [4, "Which of these is illegal under Australian competition law?", ["Charging higher prices for better products", "Opening a shop next to a rival", "Offering a loyalty program", "Competitors secretly agreeing to fix prices"], 3, "Price-fixing is cartel conduct, and serious cases can be criminal."],
    [5, "Why are digital platforms harder for traditional competition law?", ["They pay no tax", "Many products are free, so harm is not just about price", "They have no customers in Australia", "They cannot have network effects"], 1, "When users pay nothing, power shows up in other ways, such as data and advertising."]
  ]);

WB.L("shipping-container", "business", "intermediate", "The shipping container: the box that changed the world", "A plain steel box, invented by a trucking boss frustrated by slow docks, quietly made global trade cheap. Almost everything you own has probably travelled in one.",
  [
    ["Docks before the box", "Loading ships by hand was slow, costly and full of theft.", "Picture a busy port in the early 1950s. A cargo ship arrives loaded with thousands of separate items: sacks of coffee, crates of machinery, barrels of oil, bales of wool. Gangs of dockworkers unload them piece by piece, using nets, hooks and sheer muscle, then sort everything onto trucks and trains. This was called break bulk shipping, and it was painfully slow. A ship could spend days or even weeks tied up in port, earning nothing while it was unloaded and reloaded. The work was dangerous, and goods were often damaged or quietly stolen along the way. Shipping a product across the ocean could cost a large share of its value, which made a lot of international trade simply not worth doing."],
    ["A trucker's idea", "Malcom McLean wanted to lift whole truck bodies onto ships rather than unpack them.", "Malcom McLean was an American trucking entrepreneur from North Carolina who had built a large haulage business from a single truck. The story goes that he grew frustrated while waiting for hours at a port as cargo was unloaded from trucks and carried onto a ship one item at a time. Why not simply lift the whole load aboard? In the mid 1950s he bought a shipping company and set his engineers to work on strong steel boxes that could be lifted off a truck chassis and stacked on a ship. On 26 April 1956 a converted tanker called the Ideal X sailed from Newark, New Jersey, to Houston, Texas, carrying 58 containers. The container age had begun."],
    ["The numbers that mattered", "Containers slashed the cost and time of moving goods between land and sea.", "The real breakthrough was not the box itself, but what it did to costs. By one widely quoted estimate from that first voyage, loading cargo in containers cost a tiny fraction of loading it loose, roughly 16 cents a ton instead of almost six dollars. Ships could now be unloaded and reloaded in hours rather than days, so they spent more time at sea earning money. Sealed boxes also reduced theft and damage, which brought down insurance costs. Because a container could move from ship to train to truck without being opened, the whole journey became one smooth chain. Economists who have studied the period believe containerisation did more to boost world trade than many trade agreements did."],
    ["Agreeing on a size", "Containers only worked globally once the world agreed on standard sizes and fittings.", "In the early years, different companies used containers of different sizes, with different corner fittings. A box built for one company's ships, cranes and trucks might not fit anyone else's. That defeated the whole point. During the 1960s, industry groups and international bodies worked through bitter arguments to agree on standards, and the International Organization for Standardization published rules for container dimensions and the steel corner castings used to lift and lock them. Two lengths became dominant: 20 feet and 40 feet. Capacity is still measured in TEU, or twenty-foot equivalent units. McLean also made his company's patented corner fitting design freely available, which helped the industry settle on a common approach that anyone could use."],
    ["Ports move, cities change", "Container ports needed vast space, so shipping left old city waterfronts.", "Container shipping needed huge flat areas for stacking boxes, giant cranes and easy road and rail access. Old docks squeezed into crowded city centres could not provide that. In New York, much shipping moved from the Manhattan and Brooklyn piers to new terminals across the harbour in New Jersey. In London, the historic docks in the East End declined and closed, and trade shifted to ports further down the Thames and on the coast. Australia saw similar changes as container terminals were built at places such as Port Botany in Sydney. The work of loading ships fell dramatically, and with it many waterfront jobs, which led to long and difficult industrial disputes in ports around the world."],
    ["The engine of globalisation", "Cheap shipping made it possible to make things wherever costs were lowest.", "Once shipping became cheap and reliable, companies could spread production across the globe. A pair of runners might be designed in one country, made from materials sourced in several others, assembled in a factory in Asia and sold in an Australian shopping centre, with the shipping cost per pair adding only a small amount to the price. This is the basis of modern global supply chains. It also helped manufacturing grow rapidly in East Asian economies. Ships have grown enormously since the Ideal X. The largest container ships today can carry more than 20,000 TEU and are around 400 metres long. For an island trading nation like Australia, the vast majority of imported goods by volume arrive by sea."],
    ["When the chain breaks", "A system built for efficiency is vulnerable when one link fails.", "In March 2021 a giant container ship called the Ever Given ran aground and wedged itself across the Suez Canal. For about six days, one of the world's busiest trade routes was blocked, and hundreds of ships queued at either end. During the COVID-19 pandemic, shipping faced a different crisis: factories shut, demand swung wildly, containers piled up in the wrong places and freight costs soared. Shoppers noticed empty shelves and long waits for furniture and electronics. These events revealed how much everyday life depends on a system most people never think about. The humble box, cheap, standard and endlessly reusable, is a reminder that some of the most important business innovations are not glamorous at all."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the main problem with break bulk shipping?", ["Ships were too small to cross oceans", "Loading cargo item by item was slow and costly", "Ports were not allowed to open at night", "Goods had to be shipped in barrels"], 1, "Unloading and reloading individual items kept ships in port for days."],
    [1, "Where did the Ideal X sail from in 1956?", ["Sydney", "London", "Newark, New Jersey", "Rotterdam"], 2, "The Ideal X sailed from Newark to Houston carrying 58 containers."],
    [3, "What does TEU stand for?", ["Total export units", "Transport efficiency unit", "Trade exchange unit", "Twenty-foot equivalent unit"], 3, "Container capacity is measured in twenty-foot equivalent units."],
    [4, "Why did shipping leave many old city waterfronts?", ["Container ports needed huge spaces, big cranes and good road and rail access", "Cities banned ships", "The water became too shallow everywhere", "Dock workers refused to handle containers"], 0, "Old cramped docks could not handle the space needs of container terminals."],
    [6, "What happened to the Ever Given in March 2021?", ["It sank in the Pacific", "It ran aground and blocked the Suez Canal", "It set a speed record", "It was the first ship to carry containers"], 1, "The ship blocked the canal for about six days, disrupting global trade."]
  ]);

WB.L("mcdonalds-franchising", "business", "beginner", "McDonald's and the franchise formula", "Two brothers built a fast, simple burger stand in California. A milkshake machine salesman turned it into a global empire. The secret was not really the burgers.",
  [
    ["The Speedee system", "The McDonald brothers redesigned the restaurant kitchen like a factory.", "In 1940 brothers Richard and Maurice McDonald opened a drive-in restaurant in San Bernardino, California. It was successful, but they were frustrated by slow service, a long menu and the cost of carhops who brought food to customers' cars. In 1948 they closed for a few months and reopened with a radically simpler idea. The menu was cut to a handful of items, including hamburgers, cheeseburgers, fries, drinks and shakes. Customers ordered at a window. The kitchen was laid out so that each worker had a specific task, almost like an assembly line, and food was ready in moments. They called it the Speedee Service System. It was cheap, fast and consistent, and it drew huge crowds."],
    ["Enter Ray Kroc", "A salesman saw the brothers' restaurant and imagined it copied across the country.", "Ray Kroc was a travelling salesman selling Multimixer milkshake machines. In 1954 he became curious about a restaurant in San Bernardino that had ordered several of his machines, enough to make many shakes at once. When he visited, he was amazed by the speed and the queues. The brothers had already begun licensing their system to a few others, but Kroc saw far bigger potential. He became their franchising agent and opened his own McDonald's in Des Plaines, Illinois, in 1955. Kroc was in his fifties at the time, which has made him a favourite example for people starting late in life. Over the following years, he became increasingly frustrated with the brothers' cautious approach to growth."],
    ["How franchising works", "A franchise lets local owners run a business using a proven brand and system.", "Franchising is a way to grow a business using other people's money and energy. The franchisor owns the brand, the recipes and the operating system. The franchisee, usually a local business owner, pays an upfront fee and ongoing payments, often a share of sales, for the right to run an outlet under that brand. In return the franchisee gets a recognised name, training, marketing and a tested way of doing things. The idea was not new. In the 1800s the Singer sewing machine company used licensed agents to sell its machines widely, and Coca-Cola licensed independent bottlers. But McDonald's turned franchising into something close to a science, with detailed rules for almost everything."],
    ["Really a property business", "Owning the land under the restaurants gave McDonald's a steady and powerful income.", "Kroc's early franchise deals earned only a small share of sales, and money was tight. His financial adviser, Harry Sonneborn, came up with an influential idea. The company would buy or lease the land where new restaurants were built, then rent it to franchisees, often with rent linked to sales. This gave McDonald's a reliable income and considerable control, because a franchisee who broke the rules could lose the site. Sonneborn is often credited with suggesting that the company was really in the real estate business rather than the hamburger business. Property remains a major part of McDonald's finances. In 1961 Kroc bought out the McDonald brothers for $2.7 million, a deal that later caused lasting bitterness."],
    ["The same burger everywhere", "Strict standards made a meal in one city match a meal anywhere else.", "Consistency became McDonald's great promise. A cheeseburger was meant to taste the same in Chicago, Tokyo or Melbourne. To achieve this, the company developed detailed manuals covering cooking times, portion sizes, cleaning and service. In 1961 it opened a training school called Hamburger University to teach managers and franchisees the system. Suppliers had to meet exact specifications for beef, potatoes and buns. This predictability became a huge part of the brand. Travellers knew precisely what they would get. The approach was so widespread that The Economist magazine created the Big Mac Index in 1986, using the price of a Big Mac in different countries as a light-hearted way to compare currencies."],
    ["McDonald's comes to Australia", "The first Australian restaurant opened in 1971 in Sydney's west.", "Australia's first McDonald's opened in December 1971 in Yagoona, in western Sydney. Fast food chains were still fairly new to the country, though Kentucky Fried Chicken had arrived a few years earlier. McDonald's grew steadily, adapting in small ways to local tastes, and many restaurants are now run by local franchisees. Australians famously nicknamed the brand 'Maccas', and the company eventually embraced the name itself, even putting it on some signs. Its success helped turn franchising into a major part of the Australian economy. Today many familiar names in food, fitness, cleaning, real estate and retail run on a franchise model, from coffee chains to car washes, giving many Australians a path to running their own business."],
    ["The franchise trade-off", "Franchising offers a proven system, but owners give up freedom and carry real risks.", "For a franchisee, buying into a well-known system can reduce some of the risks of starting from scratch. But it is not a guaranteed path to success. Franchisees usually have little control over prices, menus or marketing, must pay ongoing fees even in lean times, and may be required to fund expensive renovations. When the head office makes poor decisions, franchisees share the pain. Some Australian franchise systems have faced serious problems, including disputes over profitability and scandals involving underpaid staff. In Australia, franchising is regulated by the Franchising Code of Conduct, which requires disclosure before signing. This is general information, not legal or financial advice. The ACCC website has a free guide for anyone considering buying a franchise."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the key idea of the Speedee Service System?", ["A huge menu with table service", "A short menu and a kitchen organised for speed", "Home delivery by bicycle", "Self-cooking stations for customers"], 1, "The brothers cut the menu and organised the kitchen like an assembly line."],
    [1, "What was Ray Kroc selling when he discovered the McDonald brothers?", ["Hamburger patties", "Real estate", "Multimixer milkshake machines", "Paper cups"], 2, "Kroc sold milkshake machines and noticed the brothers had ordered several."],
    [3, "Why did owning property matter so much to McDonald's?", ["It gave steady rental income and control over franchisees", "It avoided paying any tax", "It let the company grow its own potatoes", "It was required by law"], 0, "Renting sites to franchisees provided income and leverage over how restaurants were run."],
    [4, "What is the Big Mac Index used for?", ["Measuring calories in fast food", "Ranking fast food chains", "Tracking McDonald's share price", "Comparing currencies in a light-hearted way"], 3, "The Economist compares Big Mac prices across countries to illustrate currency values."],
    [5, "Where did Australia's first McDonald's open?", ["Melbourne's CBD", "Yagoona, in western Sydney", "Surfers Paradise", "Perth"], 1, "It opened in Yagoona in December 1971."]
  ]);
